import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="0.12.0 — A credential of your own"
  description="Trace Commons 0.12.0: log in to NEAR AI instead of pasting a key, and contribute without an invite on evidence the trace carries itself."
>
  <p class="release-date"><time datetime="2026-09-09">9 September 2026</time></p>
  <h2>0.12.0 — A credential of your own</h2>

  <p>
    v0.11 put private inference on a screen and let you route Claude Code
    and Codex through it. To actually pay for the answers, you had to supply
    a NEAR AI API key yourself: find it, copy it, and put it somewhere the
    routing component would read.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>In v0.12 you log in to NEAR AI instead.</strong> The rest of the
    release lines up behind that: a tool can only be connected once there is
    a key to answer it, a key obtained mid-session is picked up without a
    restart, and the screen tells you when calls are being answered by an
    account that is not yours.
  </p>
  <p>
    v0.11 promised two more things for v0.12. <strong>Contributing without
    an invite is built and switched on</strong>, the whole path, and a
    better model than invites. The attestation it rests on is deployed
    rather than fixtured: real signer keys, pinned, checked on our side.
    opencode split in two: contributing its sessions works, routing its
    calls is refused, and we owe you a correction on that. Both get their
    own section.
  </p>
  <p>
    One thing in this release is visible to everyone straight away, invite
    or no invite: <strong>every session in your queue now says whether it
    carries a checkable copy of the model call that produced it.</strong>
    That is a fact about the recording rather than a permission, so it does
    not wait on anything being switched on.
  </p>

  <h2>Log in instead of pasting a key</h2>
  <p>
    On the Private AI screen, on macOS, Windows and Linux, there is now a
    sign-in. It opens your browser at NEAR AI, you log in with GitHub or
    Google, and that is the end of your part. An inference API key is
    created on your account and stored on your computer, and the local
    routing component starts answering with it. If it was already running,
    it switches to the new key without a restart.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Logging in with a NEAR wallet is not supported yet.</strong>
    NEAR AI's own site offers it, but it works by having the page itself ask
    your wallet to sign a message addressed to that page's domain, which an
    app on your computer cannot honestly claim to be. GitHub and Google are
    the two providers that work as a plain browser redirect, so those are
    the two the app offers. Wallet sign-in needs a change on NEAR AI's side
    before we can do it without pretending to be their website.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some boundaries we held while building it, because a sign-in that mints
    a key is exactly where scope creep does damage:
  </p>
  <ul class="notes">
    <li>
      <strong>It creates an inference key and nothing else.</strong>
      Signing in to buy inference does not enroll you as a contributor,
      does not create a device identity, and does not touch your
      contribution settings. You can hold an inference credential without
      ever contributing, or contribute without ever holding one. The two
      remain separate decisions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Forgetting is local, and the app says so.</strong> Removing
      the credential from your computer stops this machine from using it.
      The key itself stays valid at NEAR AI until you revoke it there, and
      the app tells you that rather than implying a revocation it did not
      perform.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Connecting now requires a credential.</strong> In v0.11 you
      could connect a coding tool to a routing component with nothing
      behind it, which rewrote the tool's configuration to point at a port
      that could not answer: a working tool traded for a broken one.
      Connecting is now gated on actually holding a credential.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The screen says whose account is answering.</strong> If your
      calls are being answered by a credential the app does not hold (an
      ambient API key in your environment, for instance), the screen says
      so, instead of letting a working call imply it is running on your
      sign-in.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You can also stop a sign-in you have started, from any window, including
    one you opened after closing the app mid-way through. That sounds small.
    It was not: the part of the app that knows a sign-in is in flight and
    the part that can stop one disagreed about whether they were allowed to
    talk, so every version of this screen drew a Stop button that did
    nothing, or one greyed out with nothing to explain it. Three of them,
    written separately, each arrived at a different wrong answer. The fix
    was to stop requiring a window to prove which sign-in it meant when
    there is only one.
  </p>
  <p>
    The sign-in lives in the desktop apps. The CLI daemon does the work, but
    there is no CLI command that starts it yet.
  </p>
  <p>
    The balance is on the same card. What is left to spend, the ceiling if
    your account has one, what the account has spent, and when this computer
    last asked. A figure and a sentence are alternatives rather than
    companions: an account nobody has capped has no remaining figure to
    show, and says so instead of reporting zero. Nothing colours the number.
    A low balance is not painted as a warning, because the app does not know
    what your ceiling means to you, and an account with no ceiling has none
    to be low against.
  </p>

  <h2>Contributing without an invite</h2>
  <p>
    An invite is a person vouching for you. It works, and it does not scale,
    and it means we decide who contributes.
  </p>
  <p>
    v0.12 replaces that with something we think is better: <strong>the
    contribution vouches for itself.</strong> You join with the NEAR AI
    login you already have, and an upload is admitted on evidence carried by
    the trace: a receipt from an attested inference call, certified by a
    witness, bound to the exact bytes being contributed. Not "we know this
    person." Instead: this work happened inside an attested enclave, and
    these are the bytes it produced. Which model served it is NEAR AI's
    statement rather than the hardware's, and the section below says why
    that distinction is not ours to close.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is a stronger claim than an invite ever made. An invite says
    someone trusted you once. Evidence says the trace is what it says it is,
    every time, and it does not care who you are.
  </p>
  <p>
    The identity half is deliberately thin. Signing up stores a random
    tenant id and a keyed blind index for your account, not a digest anyone
    could test account names against offline. Your account name is sealed
    under a KMS-held key. Identity alone buys nothing: it establishes who a
    contribution belongs to and grants no authority to contribute.
  </p>
  <p>
    The whole path runs end to end, from challenge through witness
    certification, admission and terminal retry, against a real PostgreSQL
    with real signature checking, and refuses every way we could find to
    fake it: a missing receipt, a receipt over other bytes, an expired one,
    a valid receipt carrying unrelated history alongside it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>It ships switched on.</strong> An earlier draft of this section
    said it did not, and listed three things that had to become true first.
    All three are now true, and it is worth saying what they were rather
    than quietly deleting the list.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first was the pinning. The witness shipped with no receipt signing
    keys pinned at all, and with none pinned it did not ask who signed. It
    now pins the ed25519 signers for the models we accept and, separately,
    the gateway signer. Separately, because those two keys attest different
    things, and pinning one kind is not agreement to accept a different kind
    from any signer.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second was that the check happened on the wrong side. A contributor
    inspecting its own evidence is useful and is not a bound. A submitter
    who checked nothing looked identical. That check now runs on our side,
    where a refusal actually refuses.
  </p>
  <p>
    The third was what the check rested on. We used to parse the binding out
    of the hardware quote without verifying the quote itself, so the trust
    was really in the connection to NEAR AI rather than in the silicon. The
    quote is now verified against pinned enclave measurements.
  </p>
  <p>
    What is still not ours: the model's name is a JSON field beside the
    signature rather than something the hardware attests. One correction to
    an earlier draft, since we got it wrong in our favour's opposite
    direction: the enclaves are not identically measured. <code>rtmr3</code>
    does differ per model. Re-pinning for a different model moved that
    register and left <code>mrtd</code> and <code>rtmr0</code> through
    <code>rtmr2</code> byte-identical. So the quote does distinguish
    enclaves; what it does not do is say which model's name belongs to which
    measurement. Binding a model identifier into the attested data is still
    a change on NEAR AI's side.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the honest claim is "signed inside a NEAR AI enclave whose image we
    pinned", with the model taken on NEAR AI's word for the receipts that
    name one at all. There is a second kind that names none, and the section
    below is about it.
  </p>

  <h2>Which of your sessions can actually be contributed</h2>
  <p>
    Evidence-bearing contribution has a consequence that only shows up when
    you look at a real machine: <strong>most of what is already on your disk
    cannot be contributed that way.</strong> A trace carries evidence only
    if it was recorded while your calls were being answered here. Everything
    from before that, which for someone arriving today is nearly all of it,
    has none, and never will.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is a second boundary, and it is the one more likely to catch you.
    Evidence comes from the model, and only the models NEAR AI runs on its
    own hardware produce any. Those are the open weight ones. Claude, GPT
    and Gemini reach you through NEAR AI by being bought from Anthropic,
    OpenAI and Google, who are the ones running them, so there is nothing
    for NEAR AI's hardware to attest. You may still get a receipt for such a
    call. It is signed by NEAR AI's gateway, whose key is itself attested,
    and it commits to the exact request and response bytes. What it does not
    carry is a model. The gateway signs for whatever it served, and one key
    stands behind every model behind it, so the only thing saying which
    model answered is the request you sent. Routing Claude Code through
    Private AI does not by itself produce a contributable session. Which
    model answered decides that, and for a frontier model the evidence
    cannot say.
  </p>
  <p>
    The obvious way to build this is the wrong one: let you pick a session,
    send it, and let the server say no. That makes the list a menu that
    isn't one, and you find out one session at a time.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the app works it out first. Each session in the queue says whether it
    can be contributed, and if not, why. The ones that can't simply aren't
    offered. They are still listed. Hiding your own work would be its own
    kind of dishonesty, and it would make the app look like it hadn't
    noticed files you know it can see.
  </p>
  <p>
    The reasons distinguish two things that feel the same and aren't. Some
    are permanent: this session recorded no model call, or the call didn't
    finish cleanly, and nothing you do will change that, so the app says so
    plainly rather than leaving you to retry. One is a setting, and only
    that one names a setting, because it's the only case where changing
    something helps the sessions you record next.
  </p>
  <p>
    "Submit all" on a folder sends what can be sent and says how many it
    left behind. Not "all", which would either fail partway or send exactly
    what the screen just said couldn't go.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where it shows up depends on how you got in.</strong> If you
    hold an invite, every session you have is contributable and the app says
    nothing extra, which is the correct answer rather than a stub, and it is
    the same code path answering it. The marks are for the permission
    question, and an invite already answers that one.
  </p>
  <p>
    We built it now because the alternative was shipping the front door
    before the room behind it, and finding out from the first person through
    that their screen was full of things they couldn't use.
  </p>
  <p>
    We would rather turn it on once than turn it on twice. Two defects in
    this path were found and fixed before it ever ran anywhere: an earlier
    revision of the identity scheme let account names be tested offline, and
    a migration meant wallet sign-up could not write a row at all. We also
    found, while writing this, that the end-to-end test had been broken for
    weeks by a database change made while building this very feature. It
    cannot run in CI, so nothing said so. It runs now, and it is what caught
    the last of the defects below.
  </p>

  <h2>Whether a session carries proof of its own model call</h2>
  <p>
    The section above is about permission. This one is about the trace
    itself, and it applies to everybody either way.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every session in the queue now says whether it carries a checkable copy
    of the model call that produced it. Not whether you may send it, but
    whether the record is complete. Those are different questions, and the
    second one has an answer for everybody, including contributors who
    already have an invite and whose sessions send perfectly well. An
    invited contributor's trace can arrive without a copy of its call just
    as easily as anyone else's; there was previously no way to know which of
    yours had one.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the mark is on every row, for every contributor, whether or not the
    permission question above applies to you. It describes the recording,
    not your standing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where a session has no usable copy, the row says which fact is in the
    way. Most of those are permanent: no model call was answered here while
    the session ran, or what was written down cannot be checked against the
    call. One is a setting, and it is the only one that names a setting,
    because it is the only case where changing something helps the sessions
    you record next.
  </p>
  <p>
    One case is neither. If we could not reach the service that issues the
    receipt, the session is not marked as lacking proof. It is marked
    unknown, and the row says the receipt could not be fetched. That
    distinction is the whole reason it exists: an unreachable service is not
    evidence about your session, and a mark that treated it as one would be
    telling you something false about your own work. It may well succeed
    later.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is nothing to press. The mark is a description, and the app does
    not offer you a button that would imply otherwise. Whether a session can
    be sent remains the question the section above answers.
  </p>

  <h2>opencode: traces yes, routing no</h2>
  <p>
    Two different things hide under "opencode support", and they came out
    differently.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Contributing opencode sessions works.</strong> opencode has an
    explicit export command, and the contributor daemon can now import those
    exports as traces. You declare a folder; direct exports you place in it
    are picked up. Nothing scans opencode's own storage, nothing imports
    historical sessions implicitly, and nothing touches opencode's
    configuration. The macOS app offers a folder picker for it; on Linux and
    Windows the folder is declared in the daemon's settings.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Routing opencode through Private AI remains refused</strong>,
    and v0.11's optimism here deserves a correction. We said the merged
    upstream fix, writing a provider entry only where the file already shows
    the tool using that provider, was the precondition, and using it would
    be v0.12. We then qualified opencode properly, against a pinned release
    in isolated profiles, and the counterexample stands: writing a provider
    block into a fresh profile changes which model opencode selects by
    default, before any request leaves the machine, and the guard we had is
    not sufficient to rule that out. So opencode is not in the routing
    catalog, and there is now a CI check that fails if anyone adds it
    without a new qualification. A tool that quietly changes your model is
    worse than a tool we do not support yet.
  </p>

  <h2>Where we're going</h2>
  <p>
    The v0.11 framing was paying for private inference by selectively
    sharing data. v0.12 builds both halves of that sentence without joining
    them. The credential is the paying half: inference you buy, on an
    account the app can hold and read. The evidence-bearing contribution
    path is the sharing half: a contribution admitted on proof rather than
    on an invitation. What does not exist yet is the exchange between them.
    Contributing does not earn inference, and nothing in this release
    changes what anything costs. The plumbing now points at that; the pipe
    is not connected.
  </p>

  <h2>The limits, plainly</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Invite-free contribution is new in this release.</strong> The
    path is built, switched on, and its attestation deployed rather than
    fixtured. It is the newest thing here, and the part most worth telling
    us about if it does not behave.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The per-session eligibility marks apply only where permission is
    in question.</strong> An invited contributor sees nothing extra, because
    every one of their sessions is contributable and that is the correct
    answer rather than a stub. This is the permission question only. The
    mark saying whether a session carries proof of its own model call is
    visible to everyone.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Signing in to NEAR AI uses GitHub or Google, not a
    wallet.</strong> That is about how you obtain an inference credential,
    and it is a limit of the sign-in rather than of contributing. Joining as
    a contributor does not need a wallet either: the login you already hold
    is the identity.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Signing in is desktop-only.</strong> No CLI command does it yet.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>opencode routing is refused, deliberately.</strong> Contributing
    exported opencode sessions works; routing its model calls does not, for
    the reason above.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Only the models NEAR AI runs itself can be named by their
    evidence.</strong> Claude, GPT and Gemini are brokered from their
    makers. A call to one can still return a gateway receipt, signed by an
    attested key and binding the bytes, but it names no model, so it cannot
    establish what produced the trace. No setting on our side changes that.
    The invite free path covers NEAR AI's own open weight models.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Forgetting a credential does not revoke it.</strong> Revocation
    happens at NEAR AI, by you.
  </p>

  <h2>Along the way</h2>
  <p>
    A few smaller things that make the above trustworthy. The admission
    witness had its inputs isolated and its coverage narrowed so a valid
    receipt cannot certify unrelated history. And the routing component's
    pin advanced to pick up upstream support for host-owned credentials, the
    piece that lets the app supply the key it minted, which this whole
    release stands on. That same change is what lets a minted NEAR AI key
    sit alongside a Claude or Codex login you already have, rather than
    taking their place: the app supplies one credential without the
    component giving up the ones it finds for itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Getting it</h2>
  <p>
    The desktop app updates itself. The CLI is on Homebrew.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you connected Claude Code or Codex in v0.11 on an ambient key, the
    screen will now tell you exactly that, and offer you a sign-in of your
    own.
  </p>
</Base>

